    text:
      "Nina arrived in Sydney on a hot Saturday morning. She left her bag at the hostel, put on her swimsuit and took the bus to Bondi Beach. The sand was white, the sea was blue, and there were surfers everywhere.\n\nShe walked towards the water. A young man in a red and yellow shirt was standing next to two flags.\n\n\"G'day!\" he said. \"Going for a swim?\"\n\n\"Yes,\" said Nina. \"Is it OK here?\"\n\n\"Sure. Just swim between these two flags. We can see you there. Over there, near the rocks, there's a rip — a strong current. It's dangerous.\"\n\nNina looked at the water near the rocks. It was calm and dark. It didn't look dangerous at all. But she said thank you and went into the water between the flags.\n\nThe waves were bigger than in France. She laughed when one of them pushed her back to the beach. After half an hour, she sat on her towel and watched the surfers.\n\nSuddenly, she heard a whistle. The young man was running into the sea with a yellow board. A boy was near the rocks, in the calm, dark water, and he was moving away from the beach very fast. The lifeguard paddled to him, and two minutes later, they came back together. The boy was tired and scared, but he was OK.\n\nLater, the lifeguard walked past Nina's towel.\n\n\"That was the rip,\" he said. \"Calm water, but very strong.\"\n\n\"Now I understand,\" said Nina. \"Thanks for the advice.\"\n\n\"No worries,\" he smiled. \"Enjoy your holiday!\"\n\nThat evening, Nina wrote in her diary: \"Rule number one in Australia: always swim between the flags.\"",
